    "data": {
      "A": "Investigate size-driven adjacency representations and faster permutation validation, comparing flat byte storage with packed rows. Preserve the source's undirected, boolean adjacency semantics (including self-loops), Heap enumeration, and exact output behavior. The flat-byte candidate measured 0.00697 s versus the 0.00833 s baseline, but was slower than the best B result; do not assume combining representations or verifier changes will help. Avoid the measured incremental-mismatch approach (0.03257 s).",
      "B": "Build from the measured best B candidate (0.00615 s): its degree-based filtering is a promising direction against the 0.00833 s baseline. Research whether inexpensive, isomorphism-safe invariants such as neighbor-color refinement can reject more impossible cases without adding more cost than they save. Keep the original permutation/output order and source behavior; measure each change against this candidate and retain it only if the full result improves.",
      "C": "Investigate whether parallel validation can pay off only for genuinely expensive searches, with a workload-based cutoff and bounded use of the four available cores. The two measured OpenMP batch candidates were slower (0.01225 s and 0.01265 s) than baseline, so do not retain their fixed-threshold designs without evidence of improvement. Generate candidates consistently with the source and emit results serially in the original order; compare total runtime, not validation time alone.",
      "G": "Use the best measured B program as the integration starting point. Integrate other research only when the combined program is correct and its own measured runtime improves on that starting point; candidate gains need not add. Preserve the baseline's observable behavior, including the identity fast path, enumeration and printing order, and graph-reading semantics. Validate using the existing harness; do not change tests, oracle, compiler, budgets, target, permissions, or source outside the optimization work.",
      "analysis": "The baseline is correct on the reported four cases and averages 0.00833 s. The best measured candidate is B at 0.00615 s (about 26% less time); A's flat-byte representation is 0.00697 s, while the measured OpenMP and incremental-mismatch approaches are slower. The source searches Heap permutations, checks adjacency, and prints mappings in its own order, so every optimization must preserve the sequential implementation's observable behavior for valid inputs. Measurements are run-specific evidence, not a reason to specialize to test values."
